Push vs Pull — my notes to explain on camera
1. Start with the one-line hook

"Who tells who when there's new data — the server, or the client?"

Push = server tells the client. ("Hey, something changed!")
Pull = client asks the server. ("Anything new yet?")
2. The analogy (say this early, it makes it click)
Push = fire alarm. It goes off the second there's smoke. Nobody has to check.
Pull = checking your mailbox. You walk out every morning whether a letter came or not.

Both get you the information. The difference is how fast and how much effort it takes.

3. How each one actually works (keep this short and visual)

Push:

Server sees something change
Server sends it straight to the client (websocket, webhook, push notification)
Client just receives it — no asking involved

Pull:

Client asks the server "anything new?" — on a timer
Server replies "yes, here" or "nope, nothing"
Repeat every X seconds/minutes
4. The trade-offs (this is the meat of the video — one line each)
Speed: Push is near-instant. Pull is only as fast as your polling interval.
Server cost: Push means the server has to keep track of every connected client — expensive at scale. Pull is stateless, easy to scale and cache.
Wasted effort: Pull often asks and gets "nothing new" — wasted requests. Push wastes nothing but keeps connections open even when idle.
Client complexity: Push needs the client to handle reconnects if the connection drops. Pull is just a simple repeated request — easy to build.
Network hiccups: Pull recovers easily — just try again next cycle. Push needs extra work to make sure no update gets lost mid-disconnect.
5. The "senior answer" — say this near the end

Don't pick a side. Real systems often mix both:

A tiny push notification just says "go check now"
The client then pulls the actual data

This keeps things fast (push) but simple and scalable (pull) — best of both. This is the kind of answer that shows interview-level maturity.

6. Closing line for viewers

"So next time you design a system — don't ask 'push or pull.' Ask: how fresh does this data really need to be, and how many clients am I serving?"
